00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:37.280
Let’s start today by agreeing on one&nbsp;
thing: this is weird. We’re looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:43.520
a dark purplish screen with little bits of white&nbsp;
floating towards the top, as if they’re bubbles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.254
rising to the top of a glass of grape soda.
But that’s not the weird part.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.996 --> 00:00:55.360
The weird part happens when those bubbles suddenly&nbsp;
move in concert away from the frothy edge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:01.680
dragged towards one corner of the screen and&nbsp;
then towards the other. The effect is cosmic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.196
like we’re watching a million stars get&nbsp;
flung towards each corner of the sky.&nbsp;

00:01:06.825 --> 00:01:09.040
And it won’t make it any less weird to know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.425
we’re not looking at bubbles or&nbsp;
stars. We’re looking at bacteria.

00:01:17.875 --> 00:01:24.160
A field of them shaped by an invisible&nbsp;
force they are innately built to respond to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:31.760
Every swing towards one direction and then&nbsp;
another is visual proof of this unseen thing.&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.487
So what is it? What is the force&nbsp;
that’s driving them to move this way?&nbsp;

00:01:39.555 --> 00:01:44.848
Technically, it would not be wrong to say that&nbsp;
the answer is James, our master of microscopes.&nbsp;

00:01:45.773 --> 00:01:50.080
When James first saw these bacteria, all he knew&nbsp;
is that they came from a sample taken from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:56.880
Portuguese beach. And on each slide, the bacteria&nbsp;
were swimming in a stark line. And usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.520
it’s not that strange to find bacteria swimming&nbsp;
in a line, especially when you realize that those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:08.080
lines fall along particular chemical gradients&nbsp;
that correspond with the bacteria’s own needs.&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:14.880
But what was weird to James is that the bacteria&nbsp;
seemed to be congregating entirely on one side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.080
of the slide. Now at first, he thought that there&nbsp;
might be some kind of food source over there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.000
that was drawing the bacteria towards that&nbsp;
edge. So when he disposed of the slide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.080
he moved on, not expecting to see it happen again.
But the next day, James took a drop of water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.440
from his sample and added it to the slide. And&nbsp;
there it was, another line. Just like he had&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:42.080
seen the day before, but the line was sticking to one&nbsp;
side of the slide, almost as if they were being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:50.240
pulled by some invisible string in that direction.
And that gave James an idea. He took out his phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:56.240
and opened up his compass app. Then he placed&nbsp;
the phone on the microscope stage to see what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:03.690
direction the bacteria were swimming in. And he&nbsp;
found that the bacteria were all swimming north.&nbsp;

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:13.040
So if we’ve seen this line of bacteria that’s all&nbsp;
swimming north, what’s with the other things we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.755
been watching, where the bacteria look like&nbsp;
they’re being dragged in other directions?&nbsp;

00:03:17.755 --> 00:03:22.720
Well, as soon as James realized that his bacteria&nbsp;
were swimming north, he decided to go and grab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.640
some magnets to see if he could trick them into&nbsp;
thinking some other direction is north instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.320
So he held the north end of the magnet close to&nbsp;
the slide, and the bacteria began to migrate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.480
drawn in the direction of the magnet.
And then, he rotated the magnet so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.040
that the south end pointed closer,&nbsp;
driving the bacteria to swim away.&nbsp;

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:48.880
What James had found—what we are seeing&nbsp;
in action—are magnetotactic bacteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:54.106
or bacteria whose movements are&nbsp;
controlled by the earth’s magnetic field.&nbsp;

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:01.440
Magnetotactic bacteria were first described in&nbsp;
1963 by an Italian scientist named Salvatore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:07.440
Bellini, who found them living in freshwater.&nbsp;
And when he saw them move with the same singular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.160
northward focus that ours have been exhibiting,&nbsp;
he considered the idea that they might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.760
some kind of internal compass within them. But&nbsp;
while Bellini wrote up his work, it was only

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.320
circulated as manuscripts to some of the
universities in Italy instead of being published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:29.360
in a journal for more scientists to find. That meant 
that the existence of these bacteria wasn’t widely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.960
known when a decade later, a graduate student&nbsp;
named Richard P. Blakemore collected some more bacteria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:41.760
from a pond in Massachusetts and witnessed that&nbsp;
northward microscopic migration for himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.400
Blakemore coined the term “magnetotaxis” to&nbsp;
describe what they were doing. And as he dove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:53.120
deeper into the bacteria to understand how they&nbsp;
moved this way, he realized that they did in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.843
have their own internal compass.
Or rather, compass-es.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.360
Within these bacteria are a set of organelles&nbsp;
called magnetosomes. The core of the magnetosome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:10.720
is a magnetic crystal—either iron oxide or&nbsp;
iron sulfide—that’s made through a series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:16.080
of processes that involve the cell taking in&nbsp;
iron from its surroundings and mineralizing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:22.221
within them. The crystal is then wrapped in&nbsp;
a membrane to create a single magnetosome.&nbsp;

00:05:23.077 --> 00:05:29.040
But as we said, magnetotactic bacteria don’t just&nbsp;
have one magnetosome. They have a set of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:35.760
usually around 10 to 20, arranged in a chain to&nbsp;
create a dipole that can respond to the magnetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:42.480
field that crosses the world around the bacteria.
Magnetotactic bacteria are found all around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.960
world, but their preference is to live&nbsp;
in areas with low levels of oxygen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:52.560
like deeper down towards the sediment and&nbsp;
away from the surface. And this preference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:58.391
has potentially important consequences for the&nbsp;
direction that magnetotactic bacteria travel in.&nbsp;

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:03.520
All of the magnetotactic bacteria discoveries&nbsp;
we’ve described so far, including the bacteria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:10.080
that we’ve been watching, travel north. However,&nbsp;
all of those bacteria share something else in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:16.160
common besides their propensity towards the North&nbsp;
Pole: they all live in the northern hemisphere.&nbsp;

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.240
Meanwhile, scientists have found that 
magnetotactic bacteria from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:25.339
the Southern Hemisphere will actually&nbsp;
travel towards the South Pole.&nbsp;

00:06:25.968 --> 00:06:30.560
This is probably due to the incline of our&nbsp;
planet’s geomagnetic field lines, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:36.400
mean that as a bacterium travels towards its&nbsp;
specified pole in its preferred hemisphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:42.896
it also swims downwards in the water&nbsp;
towards the sediment it prefers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.385 --> 00:06:48.320
If a magnetotactic bacterium from the Southern&nbsp;
Hemisphere was forced to navigate the magnetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:53.840
field of the northern hemisphere instead,&nbsp;
it would find itself swimming upwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:59.398
reaching waters higher in oxygen and&nbsp;
far less comfortable for the bacterium.&nbsp;

00:06:59.796 --> 00:07:04.720
At least, this is the hypothesis for why this&nbsp;
apparent polarity within the distribution of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:11.200
bacteria exists. But as always, there are&nbsp;
exceptions—southern-seeking bacteria found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:16.336
in the northern hemisphere, and other strange&nbsp;
behaviors that scientists are still deciphering.&nbsp;

00:07:17.065 --> 00:07:22.560
There are many other mysteries to magnetotactic&nbsp;
bacteria, like what advantages magnetism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.440
even gives them—whether it’s to find the right&nbsp;
habitat in the water, or to hunt down particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:33.360
nutrients, or a combination of different factors.
And the bacteria probably don’t know why they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.080
move this way either. It is simply baked&nbsp;
into them, a crystal that draws them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:45.204
into invisible tracks. They are mysterious and&nbsp;
enigmatic. You might even call them, magnetic.
